<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zimbabwe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zimbabwe is a landlocked country in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, whose name derives from the stone enclosures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>. Its plateau was home to a succession of states built on gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and trade, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a>. Accounts of the Rozvi Empire treat the region's gold-producing plateau as the basis of its power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zimbabwe.jpg" alt="Zimbabwe" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Zimbabwe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of them were found guilty of genocide, twenty-five, including Mengistu, in absentia, and many were sentenced to death when the trial ended on 26 May 2008; Mengistu, convicted of genocide, war crimes and crimes against humanity for the Red Terror, lives in <b>Zimbabwe</b>, which refused extradition in 2009.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Mapungubwe arose in the valley where the Shashe River joins the Limpopo, today the meeting point of South Africa, Botswana and <b>Zimbabwe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mapungubwe">Kingdom of Mapungubwe · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Zimbabwe</b> Bird on the national flag copies soapstone carvings from the ruins; one was taken by a hunter in 1889 and sold to Cecil Rhodes, and four were returned from South Africa in 1981.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mutapa Empire, also known as the state of the Mwene Mutapa, emerged in the mid-fifteenth century in the region between the Zambezi and Limpopo rivers in present-day <b>Zimbabwe</b> and Mozambique.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rhodesia's institutions outlasted it: the bicameral parliament was retained in <b>Zimbabwe</b> after 1980, the Lancaster House constitution reserved 20 of 100 seats in the House of Assembly and 8 of 40 in the Senate for whites until 1987.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mellet holds that a proto-Khoe people emerged between 400 BCE and 100 CE in southern <b>Zimbabwe</b>, and also in northern Botswana and along the Limpopo, as the San peoples Tshua and Khwe met herding communities drifting slowly south from East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
